WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.485 --> 00:00:01.994
(revving engine)

00:00:01.994 --> 00:00:04.994
(atmospheric music)

00:00:08.838 --> 00:00:12.005
(radio communication)

00:00:15.003 --> 00:00:16.839
- [Alison Laroza] The most
unique part of rally car racing

00:00:16.839 --> 00:00:20.635
is, if a regular person were
to hear the in-car audio

00:00:20.635 --> 00:00:22.435
of a driver and a co-driver,

00:00:22.435 --> 00:00:26.521
it would seem like they
were speaking gibberish.

00:00:26.521 --> 00:00:29.398
(car engine revving)

00:00:29.398 --> 00:00:31.571
Right four long turn left five tightens

00:00:31.571 --> 00:00:33.104
three minus no cut.

00:00:33.104 --> 00:00:36.273
In actuality it's a language
that tells that driver

00:00:36.273 --> 00:00:38.200
what is coming next.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.731
Right five minus 100.

00:00:40.731 --> 00:00:43.413
They really are just
the eyes for the driver

00:00:43.413 --> 00:00:46.163
before they can get to that spot.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.594
Rally car racing in
America is stage racing.

00:00:54.594 --> 00:00:58.762
You can have anywhere
from 12 to 14 stages,

00:00:58.762 --> 00:01:02.895
anywhere from six to
20 miles in some cases.

00:01:02.895 --> 00:01:04.393
I mean you're racing other competitors,

00:01:04.393 --> 00:01:06.680
but really it's you against the clock.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.398
(pulsing tech music)

00:01:08.398 --> 00:01:10.360
So inside of a rally
car, we have two people.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:12.276
We have a driver and a co-driver.

00:01:12.276 --> 00:01:13.820
The driver drives the car,

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.734
tries to keep it on the
road, without wrecking it.

00:01:16.734 --> 00:01:21.531
And the co-driver is responsible
for reading the notes.

00:01:21.531 --> 00:01:25.205
- [Alison] 50 turn a left three
tighten right three minus,

00:01:25.205 --> 00:01:26.038
no cut.

00:01:27.492 --> 00:01:30.464
So for this example, we have 50 here,

00:01:30.464 --> 00:01:33.295
so that means before the next note I call

00:01:33.295 --> 00:01:35.582
you'll have 50 meters.

00:01:35.582 --> 00:01:37.509
And then T for me stands for turn,

00:01:37.509 --> 00:01:39.761
which means you'll change
roads from the road

00:01:39.761 --> 00:01:41.224
that you're currently on.

00:01:41.224 --> 00:01:42.722
And then a left three.

00:01:42.722 --> 00:01:45.474
Left obviously means your
corner's gonna go to the left.

00:01:45.474 --> 00:01:48.807
And a three is the degree of the corner.

00:01:50.315 --> 00:01:52.225
- [Steve] There's so
much information to take

00:01:52.225 --> 00:01:55.987
in from the co-driver, in
say 120 miles over a weekend,

00:01:55.987 --> 00:01:59.656
it could be as many as
two to three thousand

00:01:59.656 --> 00:02:04.613
different commands that
co-driver is reading the driver.

00:02:04.613 --> 00:02:08.119
Pretty much that co-driver
is the computer of that car.

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:10.453
He or she is gonna get you
from point A to point B.

00:02:10.453 --> 00:02:14.133
And as a driver, you
need to trust what that

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:16.216
co-driver is telling you.

00:02:17.791 --> 00:02:21.715
- [Alison] We started
as a team when I was 15,

00:02:21.715 --> 00:02:25.882
and sometimes I feel like
I can simply read his mind.

00:02:27.636 --> 00:02:28.886
Six and twenty,

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:32.686
left six over (inaudible).

00:02:32.686 --> 00:02:35.821
- [Steve] I really trust
what she is telling me,

00:02:35.821 --> 00:02:38.445
to the point where if
I had to close my eyes

00:02:38.445 --> 00:02:40.569
and maybe not drive at race speed,

00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:43.077
she could tell me, pacenotes,

00:02:43.077 --> 00:02:45.573
and I could still drive blind.

00:02:45.573 --> 00:02:49.240
- [Alison] Into left
three, into right four.

00:02:51.077 --> 00:02:55.547
Right four long up, tightens
at T to a right two,

00:02:55.547 --> 00:02:59.424
break turn, left three off camber, 40,

00:02:59.424 --> 00:03:01.382
into a care left three down,

00:03:01.382 --> 00:03:06.100
50 turn left three tightens
into a right three minus...

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:09.100
(atmospheric music)

00:03:20.568 --> 00:03:23.651
(car engine revving)

00:03:25.352 --> 00:03:28.196
- [Steve] Rallies let me see how one

00:03:28.196 --> 00:03:30.042
of my children has grown up

00:03:30.042 --> 00:03:31.668
and how they've become.

00:03:31.668 --> 00:03:34.582
If I looked at her as a
co-driver and not a daughter

00:03:34.582 --> 00:03:38.165
or a relative, I'd grab
her in a heartbeat.

00:03:41.095 --> 00:03:43.382
- [Alison] I'd love to make
it a full time career one day

00:03:43.382 --> 00:03:45.890
and rally internationally.

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:50.057
And I'd love to just follow
in my Dad's footsteps.

